#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/id #uuid "47507b72-86e2-5980-9e59-22d59c9f7279" :question/math-academy-id "e-9918" :question/problem "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} & =(12.5)^{2}+(11.7)^{2}+(13.2)^{2}+(12.8)^{2}+(12.1)^{2} \\\\  & =156.25+136.89+174.24+163.84+146.41 \\\\  & =777.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188215 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/id #uuid "11f8e3f1-5110-5db1-83de-99d65ea76de5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9920" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left( \\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$ \n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3711c26b-4707-57d9-bc3a-ae24345bf877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.24\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416872} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3fbe09-85be-535f-ad1f-23b1ff44f89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.11\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416876} {:answer/id #uuid "8554a30d-6e9e-5d98-ae53-faadfb7e4fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.34\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416887} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfdabea-00d1-569e-bbdd-3b4970985102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.28\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416890} {:answer/id #uuid "b72f54bc-2f78-5a2a-a357-15c7b1e0a9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.01\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e3fbe09-85be-535f-ad1f-23b1ff44f89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.11\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416876} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be518d5-8e65-51e4-8c16-7752d6693d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4505d831-af23-5580-85dc-c9dcc976f63a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139782" :question/problem "The heights, in meters, of six randomly selected children drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n1.34,\\,1.24,\\,1.42,\\,1.28,\\,1.39,\\,1.44\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=1.34+1.24+1.42+1.28+1.39+1.44=8.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $8.11\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f42cb21-e60c-5d40-a114-5979a4cb5b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434473} {:answer/id #uuid "32a012c8-5672-50ff-9832-8858dc764753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434479} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd7ab18-c424-587e-b94f-4966d49cd86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434483} {:answer/id #uuid "8dec3a1d-9535-5c8f-9c8f-05ed89cdf98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434489} {:answer/id #uuid "a9620a00-1aab-52d8-ad63-8a8916da3119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9620a00-1aab-52d8-ad63-8a8916da3119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434495} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd9a0211-1803-541f-9d71-227b97979d9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d07ab9ad-d8e6-53a3-916d-ef13c3850d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-139802" :question/problem "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n7.3,\\,8.1,\\,6.9,\\,7.6,\\,8.5\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =\\frac{1}{5}(7.3+8.1+6.9+7.6+8.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(38.4) \\\\  & =7.68.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $7.68.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416864} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416869} {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416901} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e714c6-de29-55f1-9b71-cb080706ced6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416902} {:answer/id #uuid "e85f5d4a-6136-5a58-a7ce-9cb33d65e0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416901} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b91263-f5d8-543a-8a8c-c6fd7db5717b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" :question/math-academy-id "q-139814" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416874} {:answer/id #uuid "7e200172-34ca-5ab5-a0aa-e59cf6e4d94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416884} {:answer/id #uuid "8162f83a-7db2-5f3b-82a9-fd24f5cd3429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416886} {:answer/id #uuid "9432a7e4-d864-5909-96fb-bc5b42aaf049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416891} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f74fae-3121-59b5-8358-f435d8ddb989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416874} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139820" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$\n\nII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$\n\nIII. $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f80261e-f3fc-5e23-8e3f-a6acba9abcf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434477} {:answer/id #uuid "3d10fc58-31b9-561f-8371-ffbba6ce6263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434481} {:answer/id #uuid "568b0d55-78f4-5d7c-b63a-bcab6a620e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434485} {:answer/id #uuid "97752404-ed02-56de-92db-254ae5e1b56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434492} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8ef953-c0a1-5782-8534-95a941c3407d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e8ef953-c0a1-5782-8534-95a941c3407d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434494} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4f0f31-85b3-59bd-8498-64976de6e8b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d216f49f-dbd6-5da5-81b2-ff39a5445339" :question/math-academy-id "q-139833" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{X}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=6}^{10}{X}_{i}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\sqrt{{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2}}$\n\nIII. $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{10}}{2}+\\mu $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e473785-f50e-53de-86c6-0a0d56d437c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434472} {:answer/id #uuid "327520ab-534d-5508-b50a-db2d10472cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434478} {:answer/id #uuid "606a2a26-1a6c-57ef-b3cd-f1e192952714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434487} {:answer/id #uuid "933c8972-9d57-57dd-8bc9-0b6bb925355e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434490} {:answer/id #uuid "965e4b74-64fe-591d-9c4c-09d3918cbb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e473785-f50e-53de-86c6-0a0d56d437c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434472} :answer-field/id #uuid "98c8d652-8db8-5628-8b26-929615b20791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8084345-dfbb-5406-9137-a5e08b474106" :question/math-academy-id "q-139837" :question/problem "The weights, in pounds, of six randomly selected newborns drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n5.5,\\,5.8,\\,6.2,\\,5.1,\\,6.4,\\,6\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\frac{n}{n+1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{6+1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =\\frac{6}{7}(5.5+5.8+6.2+5.1+6.4+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}(35) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $30\\,\\text{lb}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06448ce2-0e18-514f-b68e-1203b53623bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434470} {:answer/id #uuid "09c7d387-d49f-536a-b195-0974f7eff35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434471} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba67d51-ba43-50e4-9513-6d222e3e8af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434486} {:answer/id #uuid "73623fce-c5b8-5155-ae34-d860b81c911a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434488} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4ce4b-dbef-52fd-bba0-53352940d2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4ce4b-dbef-52fd-bba0-53352940d2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434496} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8fe19c0-cb1a-5326-b79f-39ab45804633" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "673ddc27-8133-50ba-9995-aed461126acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143551" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{11},\\mu )$\n\nII. $min({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{10},\\sigma )$\n\nIII. $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{11}}{{\\mu }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29453521-97e6-5150-a7bc-602fae176d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434476} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdaafcf-a37f-58e8-84d1-3d0c20ba5cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434480} {:answer/id #uuid "471b22b4-5280-5ef4-b6a9-086940207f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434482} {:answer/id #uuid "9aee564c-68f0-5f6d-b1c6-b37ef059a00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434493} {:answer/id #uuid "f209a605-cfa0-5945-86f1-b2935fd1a5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f209a605-cfa0-5945-86f1-b2935fd1a5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434498} :answer-field/id #uuid "b128b2f5-8e87-5b1c-8970-0c5544ce4df2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd7fa324-de44-5c60-a60e-ad08ed2ad90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143554" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both  known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}|{X}_{i}-\\mu |$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({X}_{i}+\\sigma )$\n\nIII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12})$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population mean $\\mu $ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05bb2d48-37d8-584c-872b-45fab789e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434469} {:answer/id #uuid "22666824-84aa-5663-9208-faedd927de76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434474} {:answer/id #uuid "23ef56c6-3700-5a51-a7ea-a3fb4bd0dc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434475} {:answer/id #uuid "52fdc8a2-4716-5414-bfb3-eb64b770f20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434484} {:answer/id #uuid "be0a8e72-f874-5a97-afaa-e5dd4b41ab6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05bb2d48-37d8-584c-872b-45fab789e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434469} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd46a11-d062-5fd0-aee5-0dff1aa44f88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afcca6b7-f927-5420-8ae5-122ee674dc61" :question/math-academy-id "q-149835" :question/problem "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n2.3,\\,2.6,\\,3.1,\\,2.8\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({X}_{i}+1)^{2}}{n-2}$ for this particular sample." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}\\frac{({x}_{i}+1)^{2}}{4-2} & =\\frac{1}{2}((2.3+1)^{2}+(2.6+1)^{2}+(3.1+1)^{2}+(2.8+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(10.89+12.96+16.81+14.44) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(55.1) \\\\  & =27.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $27.55.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5851829e-e164-50e4-a521-b2e5029611e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416879} {:answer/id #uuid "61f7e4c9-a328-59a5-8c5e-a513b1c8edcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416881} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca42f7b-82c6-5808-b73e-445211c3621a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416893} {:answer/id #uuid "ab40a5fb-1670-5156-9f84-cc7d989d494a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416896} {:answer/id #uuid "f541b8c0-a1a1-5ebd-91a9-014ae312419a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5851829e-e164-50e4-a521-b2e5029611e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416879} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60edaef-c13b-5348-91f8-4e69a22c173f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "264130f3-38ba-510d-80d5-15a003677fae" :question/math-academy-id "q-149837" :question/problem "The ages, in years, of six randomly selected college students drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n17,\\,19,\\,21,\\,18,\\,20,\\,19\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the following statistic for this particular sample.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n})+4}{n}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{min}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})=17\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $\\frac{17+4}{6}=3.5$ years."}]}
